      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© NEPjOPH.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Orbital involvement in Wegener's Granulomatosis (WG) is rare and has an overall good prognosis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "CASE",
            "content": "A 60-year-old hypertensive Indian female presented with vision loss and painful proptosis of left eye. Orbital incisional biopsy suggested necrotising small vessel inflammation. The saddle nose deformity and pedal nodulo- ulcerative lesions further consolidated the diagnosis of Wegener's granulomatosis. Systemic immunosuppressant provided remission and the only relapse was managed successfully with intravenous Rituximab."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Wegener's granulomatosis should be kept in the differential diagnosis of painful proptosis with a diffuse orbital mass in an elderly patient presenting with profound vision loss. Newer immune modulating agents are useful adjuncts in preventing relapses of this fatal disease."
          }
        ]
      },
